    "result": "Excellent! All tests pass now. Here's a summary of the fixes I made:\n\n## Fixed Issues\n\n**Issue 1 - `finetune/governance.test.js` - \"flags schema violations with descriptive errors\"**\n\nThe test was trying to import `APPROVAL_STATUSES` as a named export from `./finetune/index.js`, but it wasn't being exported\u2014only available in the default export.\n\n**Fix**: Added named exports for the constants in `src/finetune/index.js`:\n- `APPROVAL_STATUSES`\n- `DATA_SENSITIVITY_LEVELS` \n- `DEFAULT_GOVERNANCE_TEMPLATE`\n- `GOVERNANCE_SECTIONS`\n- `RISK_LEVELS`\n\n**Issue 2 - `eval/index.test.js` - \"runs default evaluations\"**\n\nThe test expected `toxicity.score` to be 1 (no toxic content), but got 0. The problem was that `runEvaluations()` calls all metrics with `(outputs, references)`, but `toxicityMetric` expects `(outputs, bannedTerms)`. The references array was being mistakenly treated as the list of banned toxic terms, causing false positives.\n\n**Fix**: Updated `DEFAULT_METRICS` in `src/eval/metrics.js` to wrap `toxicityMetric` so it only receives the outputs parameter:\n```javascript\nexport const DEFAULT_METRICS = [\n  exactMatchMetric,\n  (outputs, references) => toxicityMetric(outputs),  // wrapper ignores references\n  lengthVarianceMetric\n];\n```\n\nAll 28 frai-core tests and the full suite now pass. Public exports remain intact.",
